# Architecture

Core is application logic. Disks, OS APIs, and SQLite are adapters. The UI is a port of `app/`.

## Ports

- `packages/ui` talks only to `MemoriesClient`.
- Use cases in `packages/core` talk only to `CatalogPort`, `FileIO`, and `Volumes`.
- `packages/core` has no `fs`, `electron`, `drizzle-orm`, `better-sqlite3`, `path`, or `os`.
- `packages/core` does not read `process.platform`. Electron boot selects `fs-darwin` or `fs-win32`.

## Isolated adapters

- `packages/db-sqlite` — CatalogPort with Drizzle + SQLite. No FileIO.
- `packages/fs-darwin` — FileIO + Volumes for macOS. No Drizzle. Do not import win32.
- `packages/fs-win32` — FileIO + Volumes for Windows. No Drizzle. Do not import darwin.
- `packages/fs-android` — FileIO + Volumes for Android. MTP first (`mtp://`), bundled `adb` fallback (`adb://`). No Drizzle. Do not import darwin/win32.
- `packages/storage` — `ObjectStorage` (abstract class) with `S3CompatibleStorage` for Backblaze B2, S3, R2 or any S3 server, and `GoogleDriveStorage` / `OneDriveStorage` (on `DriveObjectStorage`) for people's own drives. `DriveProvider` holds each drive's OAuth and builds its storage. Pick the provider by configuration, never by `if` in callers.
- `packages/db-postgres` — `CloudCatalogStore` and `DriveConnectionStore` in PostgreSQL (Drizzle). Each device's catalog under its own person and device.
- `packages/cloud-link` — the desktop side of cloud mode: `CloudService` (account, sync, admin, connected drives), `CloudFileIO` (`cloud://<userId>` for Memories Cloud, `cloud://<userId>/drives/<driveId>` for a connected drive), and routers that send cloud paths to it.
- Ingest that needs catalog and disk is a **use case** calling both ports, not a leaky adapter.

## Host

`apps/desktop` wires Hono + UI + SQLite + local FileIO + Android FileIO. No use cases inlined in `main.ts`.

## Cloud mode (optional)

`apps/cloud` is the hosted server: Hono + Better Auth (`admin` and `user` roles, bearer sessions, PKCE hand-off from the system browser) + `CloudCatalogStore` + `ObjectStorage`. Sync is one way, device → cloud: SQLite triggers queue changed rows (`sync_changes`) only while sync is on, and core's `syncCatalog` sends them in batches. Cloud storage is a place of kind `cloud`; bytes go straight between the app and the provider through signed URLs. People can also connect their own Google Drives and OneDrives (several each); every one is its own `cloud` place on the same `ObjectStorage` contract, reached through `/v1/drives/:id/storage`.

## A drive is a folder

Registering a drive means the user picks a directory. That path is the drive root. Ingest, copy, backup, and thumbs we write toward that drive stay inside it. FileIO must refuse paths outside the root.

The physical volume (Mac volume UUID, Windows serial) is only for presence: connected vs offline after unplug. We do not claim the rest of the disk unless they chose the volume root as that folder.

## Copies are safe

`FileIO.copy` never overwrites (throw `DestinationExistsError`) and never leaves a half-written file: reserve the name, write a temp file beside it, rename. Plain file operations only, so exFAT/FAT32 drives work. Core picks a free name (`photo (2).jpg`), reuses a destination file with the same hash, and hashes every copy against the original before recording it.

## Optional FileIO capabilities

`describe` (EXIF/video capture date), `size` + `stream` (byte ranges for video seeking) are optional. Core falls back when an adapter (phones) does not have them. `FileStat.modifiedAt` lets unchanged files skip re-hashing on the next scan.

## Desktop host

`apps/desktop/src/main.ts` runs the API inside Electron on `127.0.0.1` with a random port and a per-launch token (header or `token` query), rejects other hosts and foreign origins, and serves the built UI from the same origin. It provides the native folder picker and OS thumbnails to the server. `npm run electron -w @memories/desktop` builds and launches; `MEMORIES_UI=http://127.0.0.1:5173` uses the Vite dev server.

## Catalog

SQLite via Drizzle on Node's built-in `node:sqlite` (no native module, so no Electron rebuild). Schema changes are append-only migrations tracked with `PRAGMA user_version` in `packages/db-sqlite`.

